 ## Background
-GPU acceleration for Marching Cubes emerged as a response to the computational intensity of processing large volumetric datasets on CPUs. Early implementations utilized OpenGL fragment shaders for classification and vertex shaders for geometry generation, often relying on the "potential triangles" approach to avoid dynamic buffer management [S1, unofficial]. As compute shaders became available in DirectX 11 and OpenGL 4.3, more sophisticated pipelines were developed that allowed for direct memory access and atomic operations, enabling real-time extraction from high-resolution volumes [S1, S2, unofficial]. The field has since diversified into specialized techniques like histogram pyramids for contention-free rendering and capacity-safe pipelines for deterministic memory usage in applications like terrain generation and medical imaging [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The application of GPU acceleration to Marching Cubes emerged as a response to the computational intensity of processing large volumetric datasets in real-time. Early implementations utilized OpenGL fragment shaders to compute triangle vertices, leveraging the fixed-function pipeline's ability to discard non-intersecting voxels [S1, unofficial]. As compute shaders became available (e.g., in DirectX 11 and OpenGL 4.3), more flexible parallel pipelines were developed, allowing for direct memory access and atomic operations to manage dynamic output buffers [S1, S2, unofficial]. These methods are widely used in real-time visualization, medical imaging, and voxel-based game engines where performance is critical [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
